  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R1, R3, R12, R18, and R19 establish mechanical alloying followed by SPS as an effective route for consolidating BCC high-entropy-alloy powders, with sintering temperature controlling densification, phase precipitation, and hardness. R12, R18, and R19 further show that oxygen-containing phases can precipitate during SPS, including TiO or Al2O3 particles in BCC matrices. Separately, R6, R7, and R16 connect Al/Cr-containing protective oxide layers, microstructural homogenization, and barrier surface phases with improved oxidation or chloride-corrosion resistance. Thus an expert could reasonably propose high-temperature SPS to densify a related Mg-Al-Ti-V-Fe-Co powder while generating dispersed oxide barriers and a protective film. The exact 1000 °C optimum, named spinels, numerical properties, and detailed biphasic/parabolic kinetics would still require experiment, but the core intervention and mechanism are already strongly indicated."
